    <briefdescription>
<para>Type-erased interface for service-entity (clinic, pharmacy, laboratory, radiology center) CRUD+S operations. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Declares both a synchronous, callback-driven form of every operation and a coroutine (<computeroutput>drogon::Task&lt;void&gt;</computeroutput>) form. The HTTP layer reaches concrete controllers through this base via a <computeroutput>std::variant</computeroutput> in the services route registry, so virtual dispatch here is what makes one route handler serve all service entity types.</para>
<para>In plain words: &quot;synchronous&quot; means the caller gets the answer through a callback function; &quot;coroutine&quot; (<computeroutput>drogon::Task&lt;void&gt;</computeroutput>) means the caller can pause the operation with co_await and let the server do other work meanwhile. &quot;Virtual dispatch&quot; means that when the route code calls <computeroutput><ref refid="classServiceControllerBase_1aef2ec41694023c184286b38ab52e7727" kindref="member">Create</ref></computeroutput> through this base type, C++ automatically runs the version belonging to the real object (for example the clinic controller). A <computeroutput>std::variant</computeroutput> is a variable that can hold one of several different types — here it holds whichever concrete service controller was selected.</para>
<para>Invocation contract shared by every method:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><computeroutput>callback</computeroutput> is <computeroutput>std::function&lt;void(int, const std::string&amp;)&gt;</computeroutput>, moved by the caller and forwarded down the call chain. By contract it is invoked once on each completion path, including failures; the first argument is an <ref refid="namespaceapi_1_1v2_1_1Http_1a6199aeb84d745378b1befef28d78892f" kindref="member">api::v2::Http::Status</ref> code, the second a response body (serialized row(s) on success, diagnostic text on failure).</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>requester</computeroutput> is the authenticated caller identity, used by implementations for the permission decision before any database work is done.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>data</computeroutput> is a non-owning view of the raw JSON request body; it must outlive the call.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>_id</computeroutput>, where present, is the optional target entity id; a missing value is rejected by the concrete implementation rather than by this interface.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>The <computeroutput>*Async</computeroutput> methods perform the same operation but must be co_awaited; the route layer wraps them in <computeroutput>drogon::async_run</computeroutput> and converts the callback result into an HTTP reply. <simplesect kind="note"><para>Implementations here are supplied by <ref refid="classServiceController" kindref="compound">ServiceController</ref>; the interface itself adds no state. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="see"><para><ref refid="classServiceController" kindref="compound">ServiceController</ref>, <ref refid="classEntityControllerBase" kindref="compound">EntityControllerBase</ref>, <ref refid="classController" kindref="compound">Controller</ref> </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
